nodecode-fmsg is a rewrite of markmnl's markmnl/hermes-fmsg:

  https://github.com/markmnl/hermes-fmsg
  commit 562f45a48a6b10504c8d6ff5c677f1ec990e6b7b
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Mark Mennell (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the original is a Hermes platform adapter, so every
inbound fmsg message became a Hermes session. Here the same fmsg Web API is
reached from a Nodecode session instead: three tools (fmsg_send, fmsg_inbox,
fmsg_thread), four verbs called through eval (fmsg:send-message, fmsg:inbox,
fmsg:message, fmsg:thread) and a /fmsg command. The parts of the original
that carry over are its token exchange (an fmsgk_ API key traded at
POST /fmsg/token for a short-lived JWT, cached and re-exchanged five minutes
before expiry, with one forced re-exchange on any 401), its two-step write (a
draft at POST /fmsg, then POST /fmsg/<id>/send), its reply-all default (a
reply addresses every participant of the parent message but itself), its
topic-only-on-a-root rule, its body read (short_text when it is the whole
body, else GET /fmsg/<id>/data), its walk up the pid chain to a thread root,
and its ancestry formatting, which is what fmsg_thread answers and what the
original used as a forked branch's context. The FMSG_* environment variables
are the fmsg section, with FMSG_API_KEY and FMSG_DEFAULT_TOPIC still read;
the address the original read from the JWT's sub claim is the section's
address, and still read from the claim when the section leaves it empty.

What was left out, and why: the Hermes platform life cycle itself -- the
WebSocket at /fmsg/ws and the inbox catch-up after a restart -- because a
Nodecode cell answers a session rather than standing in for one, so reading is
on demand (fmsg_inbox, fmsg_thread). Attachments (POST /fmsg/<id>/attach,
GET /fmsg/<id>/attach/<name>) and mark-read are not wired: fmsg_inbox reads
them and flags unread, but nothing downloads or acks a file. The
thread-to-session branch bookkeeping (branch keys, fork detection, per-chat
state written to disk) is not carried: there are no sessions to route into,
so fmsg_thread walks the ancestry each time it is asked. The allowlist
(FMSG_ALLOWED_USERS, FMSG_ALLOW_ALL_USERS) and the home channel are not
carried, because whoever reaches this cell is the operator. The quiet
display.platforms write into the host's config on first load is the section
here. LICENSE is the original repository's LICENSE, unchanged.
